00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.600
So by virtue of this being
a talk about health care,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.060
I'm going to assume most
people here know what HIPAA is.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.740
But in case you're
not aware of it,

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.770
it's US legislation that
provides data privacy

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.750
and security provisions
for the handling

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.340
of medical information.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:22.370
Importantly, there
is no certification

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.830
recognized by the Department
of Health and Human Services

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:26.570
for HIPAA compliance.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:28.310
And what that means
is I can't go out

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:30.740
and you can't go out
to some third party

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:33.770
and get a nice certificate that
says, this is HIPAA compliant,

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:35.660
and bring that
around to everybody.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:40.430
So your HIPAA compliance is
really a shared responsibility

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:42.800
on your part-- well, it's a
responsibility on your part,

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:44.991
and when you're working
with the cloud provider,

00:02:44.991 --> 00:02:47.240
it's a shared responsibility
between you and the cloud

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:49.850
provider.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:53.800
What that means is customers
are responsible for securing

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:58.030
their data and the content
that they bring to the cloud,

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:00.250
and Google is
responsible for securing

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:05.184
the infrastructure on which
that data is stored, analyzed,

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:05.725
and consumed.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.150
This is a high-level diagram
of the spectrum of the shared

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:16.555
responsibility model
across SaaS, PaaS,

00:03:16.555 --> 00:03:18.370
Infrastructure as a Service.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:20.532
And as you go from
the SaaS environment

00:03:20.532 --> 00:03:22.240
all the way down to
the IaaS environment,

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.460
the ratio of
responsibility changes.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:29.805
So when you look at a SaaS
model, like Gmail or Drive,

00:03:29.805 --> 00:03:31.930
ultimately the customer is
responsible for the data

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:34.480
and who has access to
the data, but Google

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.730
has much greater responsibility
on securing everything

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:37.720
underneath it.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.960
But as you move
to the IaaS model,

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.710
Google has the
responsibility for securing

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:43.870
the infrastructure, but
you, as the consumer,

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:45.730
are responsible
for the operating

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:47.560
system that you
install on top of it,

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.960
how you configure your
firewalls, and beyond.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.460
I'm going to talk about these
terms a fair bit in the talk,

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.680
so I'm just going to
quickly define them

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.690
in case anybody is not
familiar with them.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:02.640
Protected health
information is really

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.640
health information that
includes demographic info,

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.590
as well as information
that refers

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.650
to past, present, and
future medical information,

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:14.350
as well as the provision
of that health care,

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:17.269
and finally, also
the billing of that.

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:21.230
A covered entity is one
of, really, three things.

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:24.440
It's either a health plan, so an
insurance provider, or a health

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:27.940
care provider-- so a doctor,
physician, nurse, or hospital

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:32.920
system, or a health
care clearinghouse.

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:36.520
And finally, a
business associate

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.460
is an institution or a person
who works with a covered

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.740
entity, does work
on their behalf,

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:45.250
and as part of that work,
protected health information

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:46.360
is disclosed.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:49.450
And in order for a covered
entity and a business associate

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:52.840
to work together, they
have to execute a Business

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:56.650
Associate Agreement that governs
how that transfer of data

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:00.231
is going to be managed
and controlled.

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:06.292
So this is an important point
I want to kind of sell home,

00:05:06.292 --> 00:05:08.250
because I feel like not
everybody is well aware

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:08.749
of this.

00:05:08.749 --> 00:05:11.730
But Google will enter into a
Business Associate Agreement

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:15.450
with customers as
necessary under HIPAA.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.100
So in this context, if
you're a hospital system,

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:20.100
you're the covered entity,
Google's the business

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.410
associate, and we will
sign that agreement

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.540
so that you can bring
protected health

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.120
information to the cloud.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.790
And we're able to sign this
because of our approach

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:32.920
to security and privacy design.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:35.170
The cloud platform was
built under the guidance

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:38.770
of an engineering team of
more than 700 security--

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:41.420
over a 700 person
security engineering team.

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:45.100
Which is larger, and most
likely significantly larger,

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:48.730
than what most people
can have on premise.

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:53.470
And furthermore, our approach
to security, privacy, data

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:55.870
protection is well
documented in a number

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:58.780
of white papers,
design overviews

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:04.250
that you can go to this website,
our security page, and view.

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:06.520
In fact, earlier in
January we published

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:09.070
a well-documented
security design.

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:11.380
I would highly encourage
you to go review this--

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:13.450
it's really quite detailed--

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:16.000
and then share it with
anybody in your organization

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.700
who you think really needs
to understand the security

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:22.417
practices that Google
brings to bear in the cloud.

00:06:22.417 --> 00:06:23.750
We're really excited about this.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.669
It got well over 50,000 views
in the first couple of days.

00:06:26.669 --> 00:06:27.460
It's worth looking.

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:33.170
It's worth sharing with anybody
who has an interest in that.

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:35.330
But it's all well and
good that we actually

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:40.040
perform these security practices
and that we document them,

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:44.820
but you as customers also want
to verify that, to validate it.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.640
And so as part of
that, we participate

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:49.250
in a number of external
third-party audits

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:55.400
on an annual basis to get SOC
2 compliance, ISO 27001, 27017,

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:56.300
so on.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:58.400
So that way you can
know that somebody else

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:02.180
has gone in and verified the
designs and the practices

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:03.500
that we say.

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:06.410
As I mentioned before,
there is no certifying party

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:08.990
for HIPAA compliance,
so I can't put that

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:10.580
on that list over there.

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:13.520
But it's because of
these compliance audits

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:17.240
that we go through, as well as
our own security and privacy

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.730
designs, that we feel
comfortable being

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:22.684
able to offer a Business
Associates Agreement.

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:27.400
The Business
Associates Agreement

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.350
covers a number of things.

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:32.980
In particular, it covers the
infrastructure-- all regions,

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:35.200
zones, network paths,
points of presence--

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:39.340
for these related services
that are listed here.

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:42.410
There are 10 services right now.

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:45.110
It's grown significantly
over the past year or two

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.450
and will continue
to grow over time.

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:49.710
And when you sign the
Business Associate Agreement,

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:52.180
it covers your utilization
of these products.

00:07:56.754 --> 00:07:59.170
There are a couple of really
unique, interesting features,

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:04.420
though, as a result of
our security practices

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:06.640
feeding into our BAA.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:09.640
And because the BAA covers
the infrastructure underlying

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:14.320
these products and not just a
secured set of infrastructure

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:17.320
to run just that in a
HIPAA compliant fashion,

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:20.390
it means you don't have
any regional restrictions.

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:22.000
So you get security,
operational,

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.940
and scalability benefits
of having access

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:27.190
to the entire infrastructure.

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:30.050
It means you can have
multi-regional redundancy.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:31.600
So if, for instance,
you're storing

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:34.270
a lot of healthcare
data in the cloud,

00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:36.909
you don't have to be
limited to one data center.

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:39.640
So you can store the data
in GCS on the East Coast

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:41.110
and on the West
Coast, and if you

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:44.140
have temporary unavailability,
you can fail over from one

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:47.800
to the other relatively easily.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:49.300
Furthermore, as
part of this, you

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:52.040
can use any of the
VM instance types.

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:54.850
So you can take advantage
of preemptible VMs, which

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:57.610
are very useful in terms
of saving a lot of money

00:08:57.610 --> 00:08:59.350
for batch workloads,
where you're

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:02.110
tolerant to the VM
coming down and being

00:09:02.110 --> 00:09:05.390
able to easily restart it.

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:08.300
With all that in
mind, and because we

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:11.600
don't have to actually build
out and maintain a completely

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:13.730
separate infrastructure
stack in order

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:16.610
to offer you a HIPAA
compliant environment,

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:18.440
we can offer the same pricing.

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:22.250
There are no additional
fees, charges, taxes,

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:24.650
commitments that you
have to make in order

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:27.650
to operate under the BAA.

00:09:30.435 --> 00:09:32.060
Let me talk a little
bit now about some

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:36.122
of the specific customer
responsibilities.

00:09:36.122 --> 00:09:37.830
I have two questions
that you really need

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:40.540
to answer before we move on.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:43.470
And the first one is, you
need to answer for yourself--

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:45.420
are you a covered
entity, or a business

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:48.120
associate of a covered entity?

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:49.560
And then you also
need to answer,

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:52.200
does the workload that
you want to bring to cloud

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:56.400
require that you have a Business
Associate Agreement with GCP?

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:58.450
I can't answer these
questions for you.

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:00.912
Hopefully you already
know these answers.

00:10:00.912 --> 00:10:02.370
And if you don't,
I would encourage

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:05.280
you to seek legal advice.

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:09.240
Now, if you answered yes
to both of those questions,

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:12.005
this is what needs
to happen first--

00:10:12.005 --> 00:10:13.380
first thing you
need to do is you

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.990
need to execute a
cloud platform BAA.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:19.770
And that's done in the context
of a larger services agreement.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:22.170
And then for that environment
that you're working in,

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:25.241
you need to ensure that you
can disable or otherwise ensure

00:10:25.241 --> 00:10:27.240
that you're not using
Google Cloud products that

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.760
are not explicitly
covered by the BAA

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:32.397
when you're handling PHI.

00:10:32.397 --> 00:10:34.980
And finally-- and this goes back
to that shared responsibility

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:35.850
model--

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:37.920
you need to ensure that
anything that you build

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:41.100
on top of this
infrastructure also meets

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:42.450
the HIPAA requirements.

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:44.910
So any applications
that you might install,

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:49.480
as well as your use
of that application.

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:53.110
So let's talk a little bit
more about individual best

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:54.800
practices.

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:59.416
I'm going to start off with
identity and access management.

00:10:59.416 --> 00:11:01.040
The first real
principle to be aware of

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:02.840
is this principle
of least privilege.

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:04.790
And this is the idea
that nobody should

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:07.910
be granted permissions
beyond what they absolutely

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:09.710
need to get their job done.

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:11.960
So you should not be granting
permissions to everybody

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:14.354
in your organization
to the data.

00:11:14.354 --> 00:11:15.770
You should not be
giving everybody

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:18.026
owner roles on the projects.

00:11:18.026 --> 00:11:19.400
Instead what you
should do is you

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:23.940
should put the work together in
as small projects as possible,

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:28.640
grant specific roles to
those individual projects,

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.860
and only give people
who have a business

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:35.600
need for that those
particular roles.

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:38.390
As part of that, we encourage
people to use groups.

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:41.720
And what you can do is you
can take groups and assign

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:44.420
a permission to go to
that group as opposed

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:46.832
to assigning a permission
to an individual user.

00:11:46.832 --> 00:11:48.290
And what that means
is you can say,

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:50.720
OK, this is our
security admin group.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:53.600
And people in security admin
group have these permissions.

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:55.160
And if I remove you
from that group,

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:56.840
you lose all those permissions.

00:11:56.840 --> 00:12:00.950
You don't have to go and
change your whole IAM policy

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:02.990
for your project to remove you.

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:04.430
It makes it very
easy, especially

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:07.930
with people moving around.

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:10.210
But part of that
means you also need

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:14.050
to tightly control who has the
ability to change identities

00:12:14.050 --> 00:12:15.130
and policies.

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:17.410
So you want tight
configuration over who

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:21.460
has user management, group
management, and IAM policies.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:30.450
In addition to that, you also
want to audit policy changes

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:32.670
with some frequency.

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:34.920
In particular, you want
to go and look and say,

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:36.900
were the changes that
were made appropriate?

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:38.400
What you can see
on the screen right

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:41.340
now is a screenshot
of the activity panel

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:45.100
on the cloud console, which
is showing you an IAM policy

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:45.600
change.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:48.990
And I'll talk a little
bit more about auditing

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:51.760
a little further on.

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:54.520
And then this last
bit with regard to IAM

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:59.260
is we encourage people to rotate
your service account keys.

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:02.020
Or if you're not going to
actually do the rotation,

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:04.570
to have a plan in
place for how you're

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:07.059
going to rotate those keys
if you need to do that.

00:13:07.059 --> 00:13:09.100
And this is important
because the service account

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:11.890
key is what allows you to
authenticate as that service

00:13:11.890 --> 00:13:12.550
account.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:15.370
So if that key
gets lost, you want

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:17.280
to be able to rotate
it very quickly,

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:21.530
so that nobody has access
to that service account.

00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:24.080
Encryption-- this is kind
of an easy story for us

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:26.210
here, because all
customer content

00:13:26.210 --> 00:13:29.450
is encrypted at rest on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:32.800
We're the only cloud platform
to provide that by default.

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:35.090
What I need you to
ask yourself then

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:39.050
is does your organization
have any other encryption

00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:42.850
requirements beyond what
is required by HIPAA?

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:45.910
And if you do, we
have options for you.

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:48.175
And they run along a spectrum
from highly automated--

00:13:48.175 --> 00:13:51.070
of the everything is
encrypted by default--

00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:55.390
to the more managed key
management service, as well as

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:58.140
the ability to provide your own
customer-supplied encryption

00:13:58.140 --> 00:13:58.640
keys.

00:14:01.534 --> 00:14:03.200
I'm going to talk
briefly about storage.

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:05.560
So if you're using
cloud storage,

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:08.560
we encourage people to
use GCS Object Versioning,

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:11.410
because this is very useful
for building a Data Archive,

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:14.410
as well as protecting
against accidental data

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:17.170
deletion-- which I know is often
an important requirement when

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:20.510
handling healthcare data.

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:23.530
There is a tool that people
use with cloud storage called

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:24.550
gsutil.

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:27.700
And there are a couple security
and privacy configuration

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:29.560
things that you should
be aware of there.

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:31.643
First of all, you never
want to share credentials.

00:14:31.643 --> 00:14:34.270
Everybody should bring their
own credential when they use it.

00:14:34.270 --> 00:14:36.940
And that's important
because sometimes secure

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:39.970
sensitive information can be
captured in the configuration

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:41.810
file for that application.

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:43.690
And so you want to
tightly protect access

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:46.419
to that for whoever is using it.

00:14:46.419 --> 00:14:47.960
And more importantly,
if you're going

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:49.626
to use this in a
production environment,

00:14:49.626 --> 00:14:52.540
use a service account credential
instead of individual user

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:53.080
credentials.

00:14:53.080 --> 00:14:54.038
It's easier to control.

00:14:57.910 --> 00:15:01.810
Audit logs-- these are what
allow you to know who did what,

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:04.180
where, when on your platform.

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:06.340
Very valuable, especially
when you're working

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:08.990
with health information.

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:10.630
There are two types
of audit logs.

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:14.650
There's the Admin Activity
Log, which basically captures

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:19.390
all the actions that you
take that make a change

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:21.910
to a resource or your service.

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:23.920
And then there's
the Data Access Log,

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:26.350
which captures all
the requests to read,

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:29.800
as well as all the interactions
with the data managed

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:31.130
by that service.

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:32.980
So if you were having
a database service

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:35.050
and actually touching
the data, that's

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:36.820
where those logs
would be captured.

00:15:36.820 --> 00:15:40.900
I have a couple of examples
of typical operations as they

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:44.210
would be classified,
one or the other.

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:45.940
You can see up here
right now there

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:49.570
are two ways that the audit
logs get surfaced to the users.

00:15:49.570 --> 00:15:52.300
On one hand you'll see the
cloud activity console,

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:55.240
which provides you a high-level
overview-- basically tells

00:15:55.240 --> 00:15:59.290
you what are the important
points and makes it really easy

00:15:59.290 --> 00:15:59.920
for you to see.

00:16:02.720 --> 00:16:06.230
On the right is
the raw log being

00:16:06.230 --> 00:16:08.870
able to be seen inside
of StackDriver logging

00:16:08.870 --> 00:16:09.510
logs viewer.

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:11.990
So if you want the full
details to actually look

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:14.660
at the individual logs, you
can see them right there.

00:16:17.414 --> 00:16:18.830
Let's talk a little
bit about some

00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:22.250
of the best practices for
audit logs when you're

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:24.246
working in this environment.

00:16:24.246 --> 00:16:26.620
What we encourage people to
do is we encourage people to,

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:29.610
first, set up their
export destinations.

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:33.560
So you want to send your logs to
another location for long term

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:34.820
archival.

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:37.910
You can send them to GCS
for long term archival.

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:39.920
You can also send
them to BigQuery

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:42.350
and use that for
archival, as well as

00:16:42.350 --> 00:16:44.310
forensics and analytics.

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:47.240
I'm seeing a lot of
interest in using BigQuery

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:51.120
as a tool for log analysis.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:52.820
We also want you
to configure access

00:16:52.820 --> 00:16:54.860
to the logs in a way
that's appropriate

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:56.190
to your organization.

00:16:56.190 --> 00:17:00.669
So as I said earlier on the
topic of least privilege,

00:17:00.669 --> 00:17:02.210
you only want to
give people who need

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:04.683
to see the logs the
right to see the logs.

00:17:04.683 --> 00:17:06.349
And actually there
are three permissions

00:17:06.349 --> 00:17:07.980
that you should be aware of.

00:17:07.980 --> 00:17:10.010
There's the logs
viewer permission.

00:17:10.010 --> 00:17:12.079
And the logs viewer
permission grants you access

00:17:12.079 --> 00:17:14.660
to the admin activity logs.

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:17.060
There's the private
logs viewer permission,

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:19.700
which grants you access
to the data access logs.

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:22.940
And we decided to give
them an extra permission,

00:17:22.940 --> 00:17:24.920
because we thought
perhaps that data

00:17:24.920 --> 00:17:26.540
might be a little
bit more sensitive,

00:17:26.540 --> 00:17:28.280
and you might want to
break out who could

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:30.030
look at one versus the other.

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:32.600
And then the final permission
that you should be aware of

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:36.210
is the logs config
writer permission.

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:38.990
And a person with the logs
config writer permission

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:42.290
is a person who can set up
new export destinations.

00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:44.750
And this is a pretty
powerful permission,

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:47.480
because it allows you to
take your logs out of here

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:49.230
and send them somewhere else.

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:51.636
So you should not--

00:17:51.636 --> 00:17:55.500
the person with that
permission should be very few.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:57.480
And finally, as I
talked about earlier,

00:17:57.480 --> 00:17:59.790
you want to regularly
review these audit logs.

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:02.970
And you can do them inside
of StackDriver logs viewer.

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:06.090
You can do it inside of
BigQuery or with a variety

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:07.930
of third party tools.

00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:09.720
So we support
exporting your logs

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:12.040
to other external destinations.

00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:14.430
So you can take
advantage of Splunk,

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:16.860
Netskope, Rapid7 log entries,
as well as Tenable Network

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:19.620
Security, to use those
for log analysis.

00:18:22.170 --> 00:18:25.560
And this is my last
topic on best practices

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:28.080
before I hand it off to Michael.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:31.320
And this is what I like
to think is ideally

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:34.140
a commonsense thought--

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:39.620
but please don't include
PHI or security credentials

00:18:39.620 --> 00:18:41.730
in your resource metadata.

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:45.420
Resource metadata often
gets captured in the logs,

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:49.540
and that's really
not a good practice.

00:18:49.540 --> 00:18:52.770
So as I said-- and actually
I want to emphasize this--

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:57.060
the audit logs themselves
never contain the data

00:18:57.060 --> 00:18:59.130
contents of a resource.

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:02.700
But by virtue of capturing
the actions that happened,

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:04.620
they often may
capture the metadata

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:06.460
associated with that resource.

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:09.330
So in best practices,
don't name your buckets

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:10.710
after your patients.

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:13.650
Don't name your objects
after your patients.

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:17.020
Use other identifiers.

00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:21.240
So with that I want to step down
for a second and transition.

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:22.950
Michael, please come on up.

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:27.470
Thank you.

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:29.150
MICHAEL AMES: All right.

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:32.590
Health care people are the most
dedicated people in the world.

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:33.890
It is 4:30 PM.

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:35.930
It's been a long day.

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:38.090
This room is almost full.

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:39.270
Thank you for being here.

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:41.840
I hope that by the end
it will be worth it.

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:44.510
Now, I can't see
you really well,

00:19:44.510 --> 00:19:46.490
but if you'll do this
for me-- raise your hand

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:49.280
if right now your
organization has

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:53.610
protected health information
on the Google Cloud today.

00:19:53.610 --> 00:19:54.110
OK.

00:19:54.110 --> 00:19:57.360
Now put your hand down
if you work for me.

00:19:57.360 --> 00:19:57.860
OK.

00:19:57.860 --> 00:20:00.921
Now raise your hand
if you would do it

00:20:00.921 --> 00:20:03.170
if you could, if you could
get over the organizational

00:20:03.170 --> 00:20:04.961
and the security hurdles,
if you could talk

00:20:04.961 --> 00:20:06.890
your people into
saying, yeah, we

00:20:06.890 --> 00:20:08.341
can do this-- put your hands up.

00:20:08.341 --> 00:20:08.840
Great.

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:09.560
OK.

00:20:09.560 --> 00:20:12.110
This presentation is for you.

00:20:12.110 --> 00:20:17.450
And this is a great time to be a
healthcare-related organization

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:18.931
looking at a move to the cloud.

00:20:18.931 --> 00:20:20.930
And I'll tell you, part
of why it's a great time

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:22.310
is a year ago--

00:20:22.310 --> 00:20:24.980
I was here with my team,
and my team is here.

00:20:24.980 --> 00:20:26.330
Thank you.

00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:29.990
I was here with my team and
talking to everybody I could

00:20:29.990 --> 00:20:31.840
find-- from Google,
other customers,

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:33.410
and the leadership circle--

00:20:33.410 --> 00:20:36.815
saying, hey, we're trying to
get our PHI up onto the cloud,

00:20:36.815 --> 00:20:37.940
and we want to do it right.

00:20:37.940 --> 00:20:41.420
Where is the HIPAA
security playbook

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:43.880
that's going to tell us how
to do this in a way that

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:46.400
is safe and secure, and is going
to protect patient privacy,

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:47.870
and keep us all out of jail?

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:49.460
And everybody was like, you
know, that's a good idea.

00:20:49.460 --> 00:20:50.730
We should get one of those.

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:51.230
Right?

00:20:51.230 --> 00:20:52.220
But it wasn't there.

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:55.730
And we spent a lot of time on
our own, working with our team,

00:20:55.730 --> 00:20:57.890
working with external
partners, with Google,

00:20:57.890 --> 00:21:00.350
trying to figure out
the right way to do it.

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:04.130
And what Joe has just presented
is that playbook that we wished

00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:05.690
had been here a
year ago that isn't.

00:21:05.690 --> 00:21:07.320
And you have that now.

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:10.576
So this is a great time to
be embarking on that journey.

00:21:10.576 --> 00:21:12.200
And what I'm going
to talk to you about

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:15.230
is what our journey
looked like, and then

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:20.390
go back and talk specifically
about some things

00:21:20.390 --> 00:21:23.840
that you might take back to try
to win this battle for hearts

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:25.820
and minds within
your organization,

00:21:25.820 --> 00:21:28.095
to allow you to take this
step-- move to the cloud,

00:21:28.095 --> 00:21:29.720
enjoy the technical
benefits that we've

00:21:29.720 --> 00:21:34.920
been learning about here, and do
it in a safe and secure manner.

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:37.560
So part one-- what we did.

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:40.080
This is a little bit about us.

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:42.990
My organization is on the bottom
of that right square there--

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:44.490
Health Data Compass.

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:46.560
We're an enterprise
health data warehouse

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:48.810
within a larger organization,
the Colorado Center

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:50.550
for Personalized Medicine.

00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:53.610
This is a great organization
that was formed just recently,

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:57.240
collaborative between UC Health,
Children's Hospital Colorado,

00:21:57.240 --> 00:22:00.420
CU Medicine, and the University
of Colorado School of Medicine,

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:03.150
who got together and
said, what could we

00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:06.420
do better than we're doing
today if we put all of our data

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:07.290
in one place?

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:09.540
And specifically,
what can we do to help

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:13.140
to advance this field of
personalized medicine?

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:17.780
For those of you for whom that's
a new term, or a vague buzz

00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:21.360
word, for us what it means
is let's gather as much data

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:24.940
as we can about every patient
at the clinical level.

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:26.610
Let's then go in and
gather everything

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:29.870
we can learn about them from a
molecular, genetic standpoint.

00:22:29.870 --> 00:22:32.550
Let's put that together
and analyze that data

00:22:32.550 --> 00:22:34.860
and see if we can figure
out how to deliver

00:22:34.860 --> 00:22:38.220
targeted diagnoses
and targeted therapies

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:41.700
based on everything we can
possibly learn about you--

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:43.620
not just the
handful of questions

00:22:43.620 --> 00:22:47.250
that the doctor is asking when
you show up at the bedside.

00:22:47.250 --> 00:22:48.330
So that's our job.

00:22:48.330 --> 00:22:50.820
And specifically within
Health Data Compass,

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:53.490
we are tasked with
that integration

00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:56.160
of these vast amounts
of data in a way

00:22:56.160 --> 00:22:58.530
that our researchers and
hospital administrators

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:02.520
can use it to serve a
wide variety of purposes.

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:04.950
I'm going to give you a little
bit of company history now.

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:08.490
This is our affectionately-named
"Compass Hope-O-Meter."

00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:10.200
Because we are data
people, we like

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:12.930
to put numbers to our emotions.

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:18.810
And you can see here, we kicked
off in the summer of 2013

00:23:18.810 --> 00:23:21.810
with a pile of money
and a huge mission.

00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:25.290
And as we began to
work on this project,

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:28.740
going through the stages of
figuring out our requirements,

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:31.680
developing an RFP, putting
that RFP out there.

00:23:31.680 --> 00:23:32.550
I remember the day--

00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:34.440
I still have the photo--

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:38.640
that we were able to finally
look at these 20 thick binders,

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:41.400
with proposals from different
vendors who were going to come

00:23:41.400 --> 00:23:43.770
help us solve our data
integration project.

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:45.670
We were so excited.

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:46.980
And we launched.

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:48.480
We chose our vendor.

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:51.030
We started to launch our
building efforts in the summer

00:23:51.030 --> 00:23:55.200
of 2014 with high hopes.

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:57.071
And then what happens?

00:23:57.071 --> 00:23:57.570
Right.

00:23:57.570 --> 00:23:59.310
Many of you have been
through this cycle

00:23:59.310 --> 00:24:01.380
where once you get your
hands on the technology

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:03.780
and you start to work
with it, you realize--

00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:06.180
this is harder than we
thought it was going to be.

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:09.680
That sales pitch
sounded so good, right?

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:11.970
And now, as we're working
with it more and more,

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:14.261
we're finding ourselves a
little bit more disappointed.

00:24:14.261 --> 00:24:16.710
But we did, by the
skin of our teeth,

00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:20.190
reach our go-live in
the spring of 2015.

00:24:20.190 --> 00:24:26.100
Now, notice how much time
passed between our go-live

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:30.610
and our Hope-O-Meter
dropping to all-time lows.

00:24:30.610 --> 00:24:33.660
About three months
was all it took.

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:35.670
We were having some trouble.

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:38.670
We were drowning
under the weight

00:24:38.670 --> 00:24:42.810
of maintaining what turned
out to be a very heavy, very

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:47.880
brittle, very error-prone
on-premises infrastructure.

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:51.240
It was a top-to-bottom
stack, all of one

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:54.000
brand, supposedly tightly
integrated, and was going

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:55.710
to service all of our needs.

00:24:55.710 --> 00:24:58.830
And it was possible to
achieve that but only

00:24:58.830 --> 00:25:02.100
at tremendous effort
and expense that, to us,

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:04.350
felt like constantly
having to go back and flip

00:25:04.350 --> 00:25:07.770
on a really heavy, really
costly light switch.

00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:10.840
All it did was keep
the system running.

00:25:10.840 --> 00:25:14.370
And we were spending
resources on those activities

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:18.780
that we would much rather have
spent on value-added activities

00:25:18.780 --> 00:25:20.790
for our stakeholders.

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:24.142
We expected the server to
blaze right out of the box.

00:25:24.142 --> 00:25:25.725
We had a lot of
off-the-shelf software

00:25:25.725 --> 00:25:27.720
we expected was
just going to work.

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:31.050
And what we had hoped was
that by investing so heavily--

00:25:31.050 --> 00:25:33.600
in what, honestly,
was the most expensive

00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:36.330
of that big stack of
binders and proposals--

00:25:36.330 --> 00:25:38.130
that we would be
able to accelerate

00:25:38.130 --> 00:25:40.320
to business objectives.

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:42.730
The reality is that
wasn't the case.

00:25:42.730 --> 00:25:45.510
And that presented us
with a problem and was,

00:25:45.510 --> 00:25:48.630
frankly, quite depressing.

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:50.820
And then we had an
interesting day.

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:54.000
So this is my good friend Mary
Anne Leach, whom some of you

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:54.850
may know.

00:25:54.850 --> 00:25:56.400
She is a great--

00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:58.890
International Women's
Week-- another

00:25:58.890 --> 00:26:02.400
of these great
visionary women leaders

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:04.350
in health care, whom
we're celebrating.

00:26:04.350 --> 00:26:07.080
She was CIO of Children's
Hospital at the time.

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:10.110
And she had this idea,
she called me up,

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:13.680
and she said, hey, I just
got invited to a thing

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:15.900
to go learn about
the Google Cloud.

00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:17.610
Do you want to come?

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:19.910
At which point I
rolled my eyes, right?

00:26:19.910 --> 00:26:23.040
Because she's a visionary,
she's a thinker.

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:26.010
And the thing about visionary
people with great ideas is they

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:27.510
have a lot of ideas--

00:26:27.510 --> 00:26:29.640
they're not always good, right?

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:30.870
And she was one of these.

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:33.490
And I thought,
[GROAN] I don't know.

00:26:33.490 --> 00:26:35.410
Until she said it's
at a fancy steakhouse.

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:43.140
Google legal made me redact
the name of that steakhouse,

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:46.440
all right, but we went
to a fancy steakhouse.

00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:51.120
And I thought, OK, I
could be persuaded.

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:55.860
And so we got together and we
went to this Google Cloud Sales

00:26:55.860 --> 00:27:00.950
pitch downtown,
wherein we discovered

00:27:00.950 --> 00:27:03.270
that there was no steak.

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:04.240
No steak at all.

00:27:04.240 --> 00:27:05.370
There were only canapes.

00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:07.320
Do I look like a guy
who was happy to be

00:27:07.320 --> 00:27:08.850
served a piece of
fish on a cracker

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:10.500
when I thought there was steak?

00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:12.360
No, I am not that guy.

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:15.090
But what we did do is we
spent the day learning

00:27:15.090 --> 00:27:17.160
about the Google Cloud
from Doug Daniels, who's

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:19.290
here in the second row.

00:27:19.290 --> 00:27:22.530
And he was talking
about BigQuery.

00:27:22.530 --> 00:27:24.319
And he was talking
about Google Genomics.

00:27:24.319 --> 00:27:25.860
And he was talking
about this concept

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:31.830
of operating on a completely
serverless architecture.

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:34.650
And think about where we were,
drowning under the weight

00:27:34.650 --> 00:27:38.130
of maintaining our servers--

00:27:38.130 --> 00:27:39.780
our minds were blown.

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:43.440
Do you like my
classy diagrams here?

00:27:43.440 --> 00:27:46.530
So we started to think,
well, this would be amazing.

00:27:46.530 --> 00:27:48.774
And where was this two
years ago when we started?

00:27:48.774 --> 00:27:50.440
How come we didn't
know about this then?

00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:51.815
And we realized,
as we considered

00:27:51.815 --> 00:27:53.310
this question about
could we shift

00:27:53.310 --> 00:27:56.050
our operations to the
cloud, that there were

00:27:56.050 --> 00:27:57.570
some things that had changed.

00:27:57.570 --> 00:28:00.180
In those couple of years,
there were significant advances

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:01.950
in cloud technology.

00:28:01.950 --> 00:28:05.850
There are new attitudes
about cloud services.

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:08.019
When I started at the
University back in 2009,

00:28:08.019 --> 00:28:09.810
you talked about putting
data in the cloud,

00:28:09.810 --> 00:28:11.880
they would laugh
you out of the room.

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:13.350
And people were
starting to think

00:28:13.350 --> 00:28:15.630
about this as maybe a reality.

00:28:15.630 --> 00:28:18.990
We understood better the
potential of the business case,

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:22.140
and we had learned some
things, and we thought maybe

00:28:22.140 --> 00:28:23.449
we could do this.

00:28:23.449 --> 00:28:25.740
But I want to tell you a
little bit about the challenge

00:28:25.740 --> 00:28:27.210
that we had to overcome.

00:28:27.210 --> 00:28:28.980
Like I showed in
that first slide,

00:28:28.980 --> 00:28:31.830
there are four separate
legal institutions

00:28:31.830 --> 00:28:35.280
involved in our organization--
two major hospital

00:28:35.280 --> 00:28:38.040
systems in Colorado, a
university, and a physician

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:39.370
practice plan.

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:41.490
Which, even though many
of them have their roots

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:43.230
in the university,
are literally now

00:28:43.230 --> 00:28:44.490
separate legal institutions.

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:46.650
One does not operate the other.

00:28:46.650 --> 00:28:48.660
Each of them has their
own security officer,

00:28:48.660 --> 00:28:51.780
privacy officer, legal officer.

00:28:51.780 --> 00:28:54.300
We have a steering committee
consisting of executives

00:28:54.300 --> 00:28:56.220
from all of those organizations.

00:28:56.220 --> 00:28:59.490
And put yourself in my shoes--

00:28:59.490 --> 00:29:01.720
going in front of
this group of people

00:29:01.720 --> 00:29:07.851
and asking for a do-over just
a few months after our go-live.

00:29:07.851 --> 00:29:10.350
But we felt like-- given the
experiences that we were having

00:29:10.350 --> 00:29:13.660
and our responsibility to our
stakeholders and our patients--

00:29:13.660 --> 00:29:15.660
that we needed to take
care of every opportunity

00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:16.243
that we could.

00:29:16.243 --> 00:29:18.090
So here is how we
strategized this.

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:21.270
We said, lets do with
Google what we could not

00:29:21.270 --> 00:29:23.400
do with our original system.

00:29:23.400 --> 00:29:24.990
It took less than
six months for us

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:28.500
to realize that we were
having serious problems.

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:33.510
And we set out to do a
detailed pilot project

00:29:33.510 --> 00:29:35.370
on the Google Cloud platform--

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:38.100
in six months, in stealth
mode, not telling anybody

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:40.890
who didn't need to know--

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:43.960
while we continued to build out
and operate our legacy system.

00:29:43.960 --> 00:29:45.690
Now, how is this possible?

00:29:45.690 --> 00:29:48.390
We're going to talk in a little
bit about zero-depth entry,

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:51.390
and pay-as-you-go models,
and how it enables this kind

00:29:51.390 --> 00:29:55.470
of thing, where
traditional models do not.

00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:59.680
And we laid out this plan,
and we executed this plan.

00:29:59.680 --> 00:30:03.870
And we did it with the intent
to answer these questions.

00:30:03.870 --> 00:30:06.250
We wanted to find out, is
it compliant, legal, safe?

00:30:06.250 --> 00:30:08.370
Can we do everything
we need to know?

00:30:08.370 --> 00:30:12.780
We literally built all of the
components necessary to operate

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:14.340
our data warehouse.

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:16.140
Because we did not--

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:18.360
I can ask for one do over.

00:30:18.360 --> 00:30:20.640
I can ask for two--

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:23.160
at which point I
sharpen up my resume

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:25.440
and I go find other jobs, right?

00:30:25.440 --> 00:30:29.310
We only had one chance
to do this a second time.

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:31.560
So we had to make sure that
we were getting it right.

00:30:31.560 --> 00:30:34.020
We wanted to make sure that
the capabilities were there,

00:30:34.020 --> 00:30:37.560
and also measure costs
and long-term benefits,

00:30:37.560 --> 00:30:41.310
so that we could make a
compelling case for why

00:30:41.310 --> 00:30:45.170
our organization should
make this change.

00:30:45.170 --> 00:30:49.460
Which we did, and we
built this in six months.

00:30:49.460 --> 00:30:51.910
So you can see here
our data sources

00:30:51.910 --> 00:30:54.760
coming in from our
hospital partners

00:30:54.760 --> 00:30:56.980
and from the Colorado
Department of Public Health.

00:30:56.980 --> 00:30:59.320
Because one of the things
that we wanted to prove

00:30:59.320 --> 00:31:03.160
was the effectiveness of this
platform in integrating data

00:31:03.160 --> 00:31:06.880
that came from sources
other than our own.

00:31:06.880 --> 00:31:10.600
We load that data into
the Google Cloud platform,

00:31:10.600 --> 00:31:12.790
through Google Cloud
Storage, Google Genomics,

00:31:12.790 --> 00:31:14.320
into Google BigQuery.

00:31:14.320 --> 00:31:16.510
We transform, we
clean, we harmonize,

00:31:16.510 --> 00:31:19.630
we do all of the work
there in the cloud.

00:31:19.630 --> 00:31:21.280
So, again, follow
me-- for those of you

00:31:21.280 --> 00:31:22.779
who work in data
flow architectures,

00:31:22.779 --> 00:31:26.080
we load the data as unchanged
as possible into the cloud,

00:31:26.080 --> 00:31:30.370
and we let the
scalability of BigQuery

00:31:30.370 --> 00:31:32.950
be our friend in doing all
of the transformations that

00:31:32.950 --> 00:31:34.000
are necessary.

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:38.110
And then we deliver
reports via Tableau.

00:31:38.110 --> 00:31:40.120
We have since
continued to build,

00:31:40.120 --> 00:31:43.790
and we continue to have a vision
of building out additional data

00:31:43.790 --> 00:31:44.290
sources.

00:31:44.290 --> 00:31:46.300
There's probably a list
of 20 other data sources

00:31:46.300 --> 00:31:48.280
down there that we're
working to integrate.

00:31:48.280 --> 00:31:50.800
In addition to these
applications that we're

00:31:50.800 --> 00:31:55.930
exporting data to, my
outstanding analyst and BI team

00:31:55.930 --> 00:31:58.390
spends a lot of time delivering
one-off data extracts

00:31:58.390 --> 00:31:59.496
to researchers.

00:31:59.496 --> 00:32:01.120
These people who stay
up late at night,

00:32:01.120 --> 00:32:02.680
and they come to
us in the morning,

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:04.750
and they've got an idea
for a research project.

00:32:04.750 --> 00:32:06.820
And they just want to
know, do we have the data?

00:32:06.820 --> 00:32:09.910
Do we have the patient
population to support this?

00:32:09.910 --> 00:32:13.090
Very specific, ad-hoc questions.

00:32:13.090 --> 00:32:15.460
And they ask us those, and
we provide those to them

00:32:15.460 --> 00:32:16.480
based on this platform.

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:20.800
So we built this thing, and
we measured the results,

00:32:20.800 --> 00:32:23.540
and here are some
things that we found.

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:26.260
On the left side is an ETL
performance comparison.

00:32:26.260 --> 00:32:29.630
Now, it's tough to do
apples-to-apples ETL comparison

00:32:29.630 --> 00:32:33.956
when you're dealing with
on-premises systems versus

00:32:33.956 --> 00:32:35.330
systems that are
sort of hybrid--

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:38.414
moving from an on-premise
system up into the cloud.

00:32:38.414 --> 00:32:39.830
But we figured the
most honest way

00:32:39.830 --> 00:32:42.440
to do this is to look
at end-to-end ETL.

00:32:42.440 --> 00:32:45.200
So we start with
extracting data on one end.

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:48.170
We end with when it has reached
its final destination up

00:32:48.170 --> 00:32:48.900
in the cloud.

00:32:48.900 --> 00:32:53.690
And as you can see there, we
achieved a 50% performance

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:56.030
improvement, even
including the time

00:32:56.030 --> 00:32:59.030
that it took to upload that
data through the internet

00:32:59.030 --> 00:33:02.120
from our campus
up onto the cloud.

00:33:02.120 --> 00:33:04.820
And here is an outlier,
but just to show you

00:33:04.820 --> 00:33:06.480
some of the things
that are possible.

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:08.063
And in fact, I'm to
come back to this.

00:33:08.063 --> 00:33:10.644
But any of you operate
a master patient index?

00:33:10.644 --> 00:33:11.810
Know what I'm talking about?

00:33:11.810 --> 00:33:12.806
A few of you.

00:33:12.806 --> 00:33:14.180
We had a big win
there, but we'll

00:33:14.180 --> 00:33:17.450
talk about that a little
bit more in a minute.

00:33:17.450 --> 00:33:19.850
When I show these costs
I'm always asked--

00:33:19.850 --> 00:33:21.380
and Google legal asked--

00:33:21.380 --> 00:33:23.870
are there real
numbers behind this?

00:33:23.870 --> 00:33:25.790
There are.

00:33:25.790 --> 00:33:29.570
These are our annual
infrastructure costs.

00:33:29.570 --> 00:33:32.270
I'm not giving you
the real numbers,

00:33:32.270 --> 00:33:34.950
but the ratio there is correct.

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:38.600
You can see that having
moved from our legacy system

00:33:38.600 --> 00:33:41.180
to Google BigQuery,
we anticipate

00:33:41.180 --> 00:33:46.310
spending 60% less on
annual infrastructure cost.

00:33:46.310 --> 00:33:50.780
And we've operated this
now for close to 10 months,

00:33:50.780 --> 00:33:55.230
and we have actual data to
validate those numbers for us.

00:33:55.230 --> 00:33:57.810
Projecting that out
over five years,

00:33:57.810 --> 00:34:02.280
you can see that year
over year we experience

00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:06.240
reductions in cost and a
cumulative total infrastructure

00:34:06.240 --> 00:34:08.620
reduction, again, of around 60%.

00:34:08.620 --> 00:34:10.139
And if I was being
totally honest,

00:34:10.139 --> 00:34:12.330
that reduction would
even be higher.

00:34:12.330 --> 00:34:15.570
Because you can see in fiscal
year '20-- that big red bar

00:34:15.570 --> 00:34:18.870
represents end-of-life equipment
for our on-premises system

00:34:18.870 --> 00:34:20.130
and the need to replace that.

00:34:20.130 --> 00:34:22.080
If we were really
doing it right,

00:34:22.080 --> 00:34:26.040
we actually would have done
an expansion of our system

00:34:26.040 --> 00:34:28.380
in fiscal year
'17, and that would

00:34:28.380 --> 00:34:30.040
drive that ratio even higher.

00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:38.854
So we did this pilot, we
got these great results.

00:34:38.854 --> 00:34:40.270
I want to back up
now a little bit

00:34:40.270 --> 00:34:42.760
and talk about how you take
on the battle for hearts

00:34:42.760 --> 00:34:45.670
and minds.

00:34:45.670 --> 00:34:47.560
So we have this committee.

00:34:47.560 --> 00:34:49.780
I told you about these
four institutions.

00:34:49.780 --> 00:34:54.790
And each of them has a
security officer, each of them

00:34:54.790 --> 00:34:59.720
has a privacy officer, each
of them has an attorney.

00:34:59.720 --> 00:35:02.590
And once a month, they literally
get around a boardroom table

00:35:02.590 --> 00:35:07.030
that looks just like that for us
to bring our questions to them.

00:35:07.030 --> 00:35:10.710
Now, Google legal--

00:35:10.710 --> 00:35:12.730
I normally present
this slide with a bunch

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:17.620
of cropped-out pictures
of Nancy Reagan's face.

00:35:17.620 --> 00:35:19.950
Think about it.

00:35:19.950 --> 00:35:25.230
With all respect to our lovely
and elegant former First Lady,

00:35:25.230 --> 00:35:27.060
we call this the Nancy
Reagan committee.

00:35:27.060 --> 00:35:29.520
Because the security
officer, the privacy officer,

00:35:29.520 --> 00:35:32.290
the legal person, all have
just one job description,

00:35:32.290 --> 00:35:32.984
which is--?

00:35:32.984 --> 00:35:33.900
AUDIENCE: Just say no.

00:35:33.900 --> 00:35:34.983
MICHAEL AMES: Just say no.

00:35:34.983 --> 00:35:35.940
Right?

00:35:35.940 --> 00:35:38.580
And we had to persuade
this group of people

00:35:38.580 --> 00:35:41.670
that it was safe for all four
of their institutions to have

00:35:41.670 --> 00:35:43.950
their data moved to the Cloud.

00:35:43.950 --> 00:35:47.430
Here's how you go about this.

00:35:47.430 --> 00:35:50.460
Recognize that the risk
that they're looking at

00:35:50.460 --> 00:35:51.150
is three-fold.

00:35:51.150 --> 00:35:52.525
They're concerned
about the cost,

00:35:52.525 --> 00:35:54.000
because the cloud is unknown.

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:55.920
They're very concerned
about security,

00:35:55.920 --> 00:35:57.510
because that's paramount.

00:35:57.510 --> 00:36:01.320
And in general, the
cloud is unfamiliar.

00:36:01.320 --> 00:36:04.900
And these are the problems
that you have to tackle.

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:06.974
There is, in the
end, only one thing

00:36:06.974 --> 00:36:08.140
that will make a difference.

00:36:08.140 --> 00:36:10.140
And that is the
business case that

00:36:10.140 --> 00:36:11.640
proves that the
rewards are going

00:36:11.640 --> 00:36:13.920
to be greater than the risks.

00:36:13.920 --> 00:36:17.010
And you're going to answer cost
by demonstrating that there's

00:36:17.010 --> 00:36:20.460
going to be increased
revenue, or cost savings,

00:36:20.460 --> 00:36:21.840
by moving to the cloud.

00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:23.640
You're going to answer
security by saying,

00:36:23.640 --> 00:36:26.830
the security is going to be
better when we get there.

00:36:26.830 --> 00:36:29.640
We will fulfill our
responsibility to our patients

00:36:29.640 --> 00:36:33.000
more fully by taking
advantage of those 700

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:34.380
security and
compliance engineers

00:36:34.380 --> 00:36:35.550
that Joe talked about.

00:36:35.550 --> 00:36:37.008
And you're going
to answer concerns

00:36:37.008 --> 00:36:39.540
about the unknown
with opportunities

00:36:39.540 --> 00:36:41.820
to innovate that you'll
be able to take advantage

00:36:41.820 --> 00:36:45.270
of in the cloud that you
won't in other places.

00:36:45.270 --> 00:36:48.510
Here is part of a
helpful way, I hope,

00:36:48.510 --> 00:36:51.570
of talking about
cost versus value.

00:36:51.570 --> 00:36:54.600
Because in the end, the
money aspect of this matters.

00:36:54.600 --> 00:36:59.520
So that blue area
represents investment

00:36:59.520 --> 00:37:02.520
in infrastructure
capacity, which

00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:04.440
has this stepwise function.

00:37:04.440 --> 00:37:08.970
That red line is your
usage on that capacity.

00:37:08.970 --> 00:37:11.190
And let's just assume that
it's going up kind of flat

00:37:11.190 --> 00:37:11.730
over time.

00:37:11.730 --> 00:37:14.130
You can twist and warp
this model all you want,

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:17.520
but it gives you the same
results, more or less.

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:20.970
As you approach
capacity, you need

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:22.920
to expand upon your
infrastructure.

00:37:22.920 --> 00:37:26.070
And you make a big investment,
and you deploy out new servers,

00:37:26.070 --> 00:37:28.750
and then you do that again
after some period later.

00:37:28.750 --> 00:37:32.654
And the thing that happens
is the infrastructure

00:37:32.654 --> 00:37:34.320
that you had invested
in that you're not

00:37:34.320 --> 00:37:37.320
using-- that time between when
you first make the purchase

00:37:37.320 --> 00:37:41.580
and when you start to reach
capacity is considered waste.

00:37:41.580 --> 00:37:45.510
That's money that you've
spent that you aren't using.

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:47.880
And look how that
adds up over time.

00:37:47.880 --> 00:37:53.460
That orange line shows how
that wasted area accumulates.

00:37:53.460 --> 00:37:58.740
This has a dramatic effect
on total cost of ownership.

00:37:58.740 --> 00:38:01.470
And the model, when you
move to an infrastructure

00:38:01.470 --> 00:38:05.970
where you can scale up
more closely to your usage

00:38:05.970 --> 00:38:08.200
changes this significantly.

00:38:08.200 --> 00:38:09.690
So here's a
pay-as-you-go model--

00:38:09.690 --> 00:38:12.300
only investing in the
infrastructure that you need.

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:14.880
With systems like BigQuery,
that stepwise function

00:38:14.880 --> 00:38:17.430
is even flatter, because
you're literally only

00:38:17.430 --> 00:38:19.684
paying for queries
as you execute them.

00:38:19.684 --> 00:38:21.600
But even if you're
deploying virtual machines,

00:38:21.600 --> 00:38:24.720
and you're able to gradually
scale those out and build them

00:38:24.720 --> 00:38:27.990
up, we have the same usage.

00:38:27.990 --> 00:38:29.520
We have the same slope.

00:38:29.520 --> 00:38:31.430
But the mean is much lower.

00:38:31.430 --> 00:38:34.010
And look what happens to
the accumulated waste--

00:38:34.010 --> 00:38:37.140
it is much, much smaller.

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:41.250
This has a dramatic effect on
your total cost of ownership,

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:43.470
and in my opinion, is one
of the strongest arguments

00:38:43.470 --> 00:38:46.680
for a move to the cloud from
an economic point of view.

00:38:46.680 --> 00:38:49.170
I want to verify with our
folks that this clock is

00:38:49.170 --> 00:38:49.910
counting down.

00:38:49.910 --> 00:38:51.820
It's telling me how much time
we have left in the session?

00:38:51.820 --> 00:38:52.030
Yes?

00:38:52.030 --> 00:38:52.529
OK.

00:38:52.529 --> 00:38:54.150
Great.

00:38:54.150 --> 00:38:55.750
I was confused for a second.

00:38:55.750 --> 00:38:57.990
Second thing-- it's time
to change the conversation.

00:38:57.990 --> 00:39:00.510
So this was a release
from about last October

00:39:00.510 --> 00:39:04.530
from HHS on HIPAA
and cloud computing.

00:39:04.530 --> 00:39:06.810
The question of, oh, will
the government get mad at us

00:39:06.810 --> 00:39:09.120
if we put data in
the cloud any more?

00:39:09.120 --> 00:39:11.100
The answer to that is no.

00:39:11.100 --> 00:39:12.120
This guidance exists.

00:39:12.120 --> 00:39:14.850
You can go look it up online
on a government website.

00:39:14.850 --> 00:39:15.750
You can read it.

00:39:15.750 --> 00:39:18.420
It has very specific,
very helpful guidance

00:39:18.420 --> 00:39:22.080
for how to engage effectively
with a cloud service provider.

00:39:22.080 --> 00:39:24.120
And my recommendation
is that you

00:39:24.120 --> 00:39:26.789
stop talking about this,
like, gee, I don't know

00:39:26.789 --> 00:39:28.080
if we can do this in the cloud.

00:39:28.080 --> 00:39:29.610
But the fact is
the advantages are

00:39:29.610 --> 00:39:33.270
so strong that the
conversation needs to be--

00:39:33.270 --> 00:39:36.950
how quickly can we get there?

00:39:36.950 --> 00:39:39.140
A little repeat from some
of what Joe talked about--

00:39:39.140 --> 00:39:42.350
recognize that in
terms of security

00:39:42.350 --> 00:39:45.170
there is a joint responsibility.

00:39:45.170 --> 00:39:48.530
Google owns physical
infrastructure, platform,

00:39:48.530 --> 00:39:51.140
and Software as a
Service level security.

00:39:51.140 --> 00:39:53.027
You still own everything
top to bottom,

00:39:53.027 --> 00:39:54.860
and this is important,
again, in this battle

00:39:54.860 --> 00:39:56.060
for hearts and minds.

00:39:56.060 --> 00:39:59.150
Because you have your security
and compliance officers,

00:39:59.150 --> 00:40:01.070
they need to have
confidence that you know

00:40:01.070 --> 00:40:02.349
what you are talking about.

00:40:02.349 --> 00:40:03.890
And the moment you
go in and say, oh,

00:40:03.890 --> 00:40:04.940
we're going to
move this to Google

00:40:04.940 --> 00:40:07.010
and they're going to
take care of security,

00:40:07.010 --> 00:40:10.190
they will know that you don't
know what you're talking about.

00:40:10.190 --> 00:40:15.560
You have a responsibility
to understand

00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:17.180
what your responsibility is.

00:40:17.180 --> 00:40:19.140
And you need to be able
to speak this language.

00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:20.681
You need to understand
what it really

00:40:20.681 --> 00:40:22.550
means to be HIPAA compliant.

00:40:22.550 --> 00:40:25.924
You don't need to understand
how encryption algorithms work,

00:40:25.924 --> 00:40:28.340
but you need to understand
where the responsibilities lie,

00:40:28.340 --> 00:40:30.650
and the difference
between technical security

00:40:30.650 --> 00:40:32.780
and organizational
level controls,

00:40:32.780 --> 00:40:34.490
and to be able to define--

00:40:34.490 --> 00:40:35.600
here's what we're going
to get from Google,

00:40:35.600 --> 00:40:37.766
and here's what we're going
to have to do ourselves.

00:40:37.766 --> 00:40:39.560
If you can't speak
that language,

00:40:39.560 --> 00:40:42.470
find somebody who can,
and get them to help you,

00:40:42.470 --> 00:40:44.120
get them on your
side, and have them

00:40:44.120 --> 00:40:47.064
help you to advocate for
the security of the cloud.

00:40:47.064 --> 00:40:48.230
Here's another way to do it.

00:40:48.230 --> 00:40:50.900
This is Fort Knox.

00:40:50.900 --> 00:40:54.680
Google legal made me go back
and get a non-copyrighted photo

00:40:54.680 --> 00:40:55.490
of Fort Knox.

00:40:59.030 --> 00:41:01.340
Google has built Fort
Knox in their data center.

00:41:01.340 --> 00:41:04.070
It is literally true that the
South Carolina data center

00:41:04.070 --> 00:41:06.550
has alligators on the property.

00:41:06.550 --> 00:41:09.080
Not as a formal part of
the security mechanism,

00:41:09.080 --> 00:41:11.562
but they sort of moved in.

00:41:11.562 --> 00:41:12.770
And Google said, that's fine.

00:41:12.770 --> 00:41:13.280
We'll leave them there.

00:41:13.280 --> 00:41:14.980
And they put up some "beware
of alligators" signs,

00:41:14.980 --> 00:41:15.938
and it's going to help.

00:41:15.938 --> 00:41:19.910
How many of you have alligators
outside your data center?

00:41:19.910 --> 00:41:21.752
Not many.

00:41:21.752 --> 00:41:23.210
You still have this
responsibility.

00:41:23.210 --> 00:41:25.340
You can build the strongest
vault in the world.

00:41:25.340 --> 00:41:28.830
If you leave it unlocked, you're
going to have problems, right?

00:41:28.830 --> 00:41:31.630
So those orange bars--

00:41:31.630 --> 00:41:35.120
your responsibility.

00:41:35.120 --> 00:41:37.140
Again, what Google
does, what we do.

00:41:37.140 --> 00:41:39.650
Joe already talked about all
of the different compliance

00:41:39.650 --> 00:41:41.150
and certifications of--

00:41:41.150 --> 00:41:42.944
my HIPAA with the
asterisk here--

00:41:42.944 --> 00:41:44.360
just to repeat
what he said, there

00:41:44.360 --> 00:41:46.520
is no HIPAA certification.

00:41:46.520 --> 00:41:49.100
If somebody tells you that
they have one, they're lying.

00:41:49.100 --> 00:41:51.680
What they have is maybe a
third party auditor who said,

00:41:51.680 --> 00:41:53.630
yeah, we think that
this is HIPAA compliant.

00:41:53.630 --> 00:41:55.862
But the government
hasn't set a checkbox way

00:41:55.862 --> 00:41:57.320
to say that you're
HIPAA compliant.

00:41:57.320 --> 00:41:59.480
So there is some judgment
that you've got to take there.

00:41:59.480 --> 00:42:01.229
It's going to feel
like you're never done.

00:42:01.229 --> 00:42:05.330
That's fine-- sleep
is for mortals.

00:42:05.330 --> 00:42:07.010
Recognize that Google is--

00:42:07.010 --> 00:42:08.060
I'll be honest.

00:42:08.060 --> 00:42:11.857
One of the things that brought
comfort to our legal folks

00:42:11.857 --> 00:42:13.940
was recognizing that in
the event of a breach that

00:42:13.940 --> 00:42:19.480
was Google's fault, Google's
got a lot of resources

00:42:19.480 --> 00:42:21.120
that in the event
of a lawsuit, we're

00:42:21.120 --> 00:42:23.740
confident they could
probably cover our expenses.

00:42:23.740 --> 00:42:24.240
Right?

00:42:24.240 --> 00:42:25.156
If that had to happen.

00:42:25.156 --> 00:42:26.550
We hope that never happens.

00:42:26.550 --> 00:42:28.950
But there is work here
for you to do as well.

00:42:28.950 --> 00:42:32.280
Map those various
compliance certifications

00:42:32.280 --> 00:42:35.760
to whatever requirements
your organization has.

00:42:35.760 --> 00:42:39.120
If you want to throw independent
pen testers at it to make

00:42:39.120 --> 00:42:40.820
people comfortable, do that.

00:42:40.820 --> 00:42:44.240
In fact, one of the early things
that we sort of said, wow,

00:42:44.240 --> 00:42:46.634
I think we're going down
the right path is-- again,

00:42:46.634 --> 00:42:47.550
I asked Doug Daniels--

00:42:47.550 --> 00:42:50.880
I said, if we wanted to
have our in-house white hat

00:42:50.880 --> 00:42:52.830
hackers go after
this thing and try

00:42:52.830 --> 00:42:55.129
to penetrate BigQuery,
or something like that,

00:42:55.129 --> 00:42:55.920
could they do that?

00:42:55.920 --> 00:42:57.045
Or would we get in trouble?

00:42:57.045 --> 00:42:59.580
And he's like, go for it.

00:42:59.580 --> 00:43:02.610
Because Google is doing
this always themselves.

00:43:02.610 --> 00:43:04.710
They're giving out
money to people

00:43:04.710 --> 00:43:07.020
who are trying to
hack their system.

00:43:07.020 --> 00:43:09.585
You can build hybrid monitoring
and alerting systems.

00:43:09.585 --> 00:43:11.210
If you have local
systems, or if you're

00:43:11.210 --> 00:43:14.190
using Splunk, or
Nagios, or other systems

00:43:14.190 --> 00:43:18.521
that your security people
are comfortable with--

00:43:18.521 --> 00:43:19.020
fine.

00:43:19.020 --> 00:43:21.420
Let them have those things.

00:43:21.420 --> 00:43:25.380
And build mechanisms that allow
them to use hybrid approaches

00:43:25.380 --> 00:43:27.420
to monitoring and alerting.

00:43:27.420 --> 00:43:29.214
And watch the contracts.

00:43:29.214 --> 00:43:30.630
I was going to
make a whole slide,

00:43:30.630 --> 00:43:32.004
but there's probably
another hour

00:43:32.004 --> 00:43:34.530
on making sure that
your contracts are

00:43:34.530 --> 00:43:39.430
unambiguous and appropriate
liabilities are set up.

00:43:39.430 --> 00:43:42.160
Finally, to the
innovation point.

00:43:42.160 --> 00:43:45.430
Please think beyond
the lift and shift--

00:43:45.430 --> 00:43:48.490
let's get our virtual machines
out of our data center

00:43:48.490 --> 00:43:49.300
and onto Google.

00:43:49.300 --> 00:43:50.800
There have been a
lot of discussions

00:43:50.800 --> 00:43:55.510
here and in the keynotes about
how you do this incrementally.

00:43:55.510 --> 00:43:57.520
Fine to take an
incremental approach,

00:43:57.520 --> 00:44:00.835
but don't get your VMs
out of your data center

00:44:00.835 --> 00:44:02.710
and into Google's and
consider yourself done.

00:44:02.710 --> 00:44:07.720
Because the greatest gains are
to be had in the cloud 2.0,

00:44:07.720 --> 00:44:08.890
cloud 3.0--

00:44:08.890 --> 00:44:12.800
thinking beyond virtual
machines and into SaaS and PaaS

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:13.300
offerings.

00:44:13.300 --> 00:44:15.130
So here's one.

00:44:15.130 --> 00:44:18.250
Couple of super
smart guys on my team

00:44:18.250 --> 00:44:20.800
here work together to see--

00:44:20.800 --> 00:44:23.110
hey, every time
we do a data load

00:44:23.110 --> 00:44:25.450
we have to take these six
million patient records

00:44:25.450 --> 00:44:28.870
and do this big fuzzy
match cross-join.

00:44:28.870 --> 00:44:32.500
We want to find out if Bobby
Peterson at the Children's

00:44:32.500 --> 00:44:36.550
Hospital is the same as
Roberta Peterson at the adult

00:44:36.550 --> 00:44:39.460
hospital with
10-year-apart visits.

00:44:39.460 --> 00:44:40.800
That's a tricky problem.

00:44:40.800 --> 00:44:42.956
It's a huge
computational challenge.

00:44:42.956 --> 00:44:44.830
And any of you who
operate systems like this,

00:44:44.830 --> 00:44:48.460
you know it's not
a small effort.

00:44:48.460 --> 00:44:53.300
Our legacy system had
an expensive application

00:44:53.300 --> 00:44:55.480
and server that this
ran on, and to do

00:44:55.480 --> 00:44:58.330
that six-million-record
fuzzy cross-join

00:44:58.330 --> 00:45:00.070
took about eight hours.

00:45:00.070 --> 00:45:03.070
They reimplemented
this in BigQuery,

00:45:03.070 --> 00:45:04.900
using BigQuery's user
defined functions,

00:45:04.900 --> 00:45:08.980
using JavaScript in order
to do the fuzzy matching.

00:45:08.980 --> 00:45:11.860
Ran it, and it finished
in about 15 minutes,

00:45:11.860 --> 00:45:15.430
and it cost us about $1.50
every time we ran it,

00:45:15.430 --> 00:45:19.480
in terms of BigQuery
computational power.

00:45:19.480 --> 00:45:22.390
That is the kind
of win that you can

00:45:22.390 --> 00:45:26.530
get when you look beyond just
moving your virtual machines

00:45:26.530 --> 00:45:28.630
into the sky and
say, what can we

00:45:28.630 --> 00:45:35.110
do with some of these
scalable, serverless tools that

00:45:35.110 --> 00:45:37.750
are out there?

00:45:37.750 --> 00:45:42.220
What could you do if suddenly
you didn't have to keep asking,

00:45:42.220 --> 00:45:44.230
do we have enough storage space?

00:45:44.230 --> 00:45:46.480
Do we have enough compute power?

00:45:46.480 --> 00:45:48.970
What could you do
with zero-depth entry?

00:45:48.970 --> 00:45:52.750
That six month pilot
started in about March.

00:45:52.750 --> 00:45:56.140
In about June, my
wife came upstairs

00:45:56.140 --> 00:45:59.230
and said, why do we have
a multi-thousand dollar

00:45:59.230 --> 00:46:01.660
bill to Google on our
personal credit card?

00:46:04.300 --> 00:46:08.680
Because back in March, I sat
on my couch, in my pajamas,

00:46:08.680 --> 00:46:10.430
and signed up for
our Google account

00:46:10.430 --> 00:46:12.550
on my personal credit
card and didn't notice it

00:46:12.550 --> 00:46:13.540
for three months.

00:46:13.540 --> 00:46:16.360
Because as we were starting
to load data in and work

00:46:16.360 --> 00:46:17.890
with the system in
this stealth mode

00:46:17.890 --> 00:46:21.330
project that costs
were negligible.

00:46:21.330 --> 00:46:23.080
And it wasn't until
we started deploying

00:46:23.080 --> 00:46:24.860
lots of data and lots
of virtual machines

00:46:24.860 --> 00:46:28.480
that it even hit the radar
on the credit card bill.

00:46:28.480 --> 00:46:31.270
Don't do that.

00:46:31.270 --> 00:46:34.630
Google legal doesn't make
it easy to get that back.

00:46:34.630 --> 00:46:35.710
But we did get it back.

00:46:35.710 --> 00:46:38.800
Thank you again, Doug Daniels,
and apologies to my wife.

00:46:38.800 --> 00:46:40.042
So we got that done.

00:46:40.042 --> 00:46:41.500
But the point is,
the fact that you

00:46:41.500 --> 00:46:43.990
can make that
mistake accidentally

00:46:43.990 --> 00:46:48.160
certainly means that you
won't need CFO authorization

00:46:48.160 --> 00:46:52.300
and a big CAPEX spend in
order to start playing around.

00:46:52.300 --> 00:46:55.000
I think everybody here
has got a $300 credit.

00:46:55.000 --> 00:46:57.820
I think they've extended
the period from six months

00:46:57.820 --> 00:47:00.160
to 12 months for
the trial period.

00:47:00.160 --> 00:47:03.760
Get in and start with this
zero-depth entry approach,

00:47:03.760 --> 00:47:05.020
seeing what you can do.

00:47:05.020 --> 00:47:07.380
Think about cloud-first
architectures.

00:47:07.380 --> 00:47:09.490
So we're taking our
data, we're pushing it

00:47:09.490 --> 00:47:13.600
into the cloud unchanged, and
we're doing all the work there.

00:47:13.600 --> 00:47:16.210
What could you do if people
could get to your data

00:47:16.210 --> 00:47:18.790
from all over the region
or all over the world?

00:47:18.790 --> 00:47:21.310
What can you do with the
integration between your data

00:47:21.310 --> 00:47:25.900
in the cloud and these amazing
cloud-based analytical tools

00:47:25.900 --> 00:47:27.010
that are out there?

00:47:27.010 --> 00:47:29.980
And given that you will
hopefully save money, what do

00:47:29.980 --> 00:47:32.650
you do with that?

00:47:32.650 --> 00:47:35.380
You take that money,
you don't give it back,

00:47:35.380 --> 00:47:38.200
and you redirect that toward
value-added activities.

00:47:38.200 --> 00:47:40.270
For us that means
hiring more analysts,

00:47:40.270 --> 00:47:42.790
bringing in more
data sources, working

00:47:42.790 --> 00:47:45.220
on improving our customer
relationship management

00:47:45.220 --> 00:47:48.520
approaches, and building
a better organization that

00:47:48.520 --> 00:47:53.095
is much more focused
on delivering value.

00:47:53.095 --> 00:47:54.700
I'm not going to
repeat all this.

00:47:54.700 --> 00:47:56.740
This is summarizing
what we just said.

00:47:56.740 --> 00:47:58.450
You've got to build
the business case.

00:47:58.450 --> 00:48:00.908
You've got to make sure that
you can speak to the security.

00:48:00.908 --> 00:48:04.660
And you've got to
focus on innovation.

00:48:04.660 --> 00:48:06.780
That's all I have by
way of information.

00:48:06.780 --> 00:48:09.300
I wanted to give some thank
yous to the folks on the screen

00:48:09.300 --> 00:48:11.970
here who really helped us in
building these things out,

00:48:11.970 --> 00:48:14.250
figuring out our
security approaches,

00:48:14.250 --> 00:48:17.250
helping us with processes,
architecture design.

00:48:17.250 --> 00:48:19.470
Huge thanks to my team,
most of whom are here,

00:48:19.470 --> 00:48:23.320
and who really were the
hands on doing this work.

00:48:23.320 --> 00:48:25.070
And let me just ask
Joe-- do we have time?

00:48:25.070 --> 00:48:26.160
Do we want to hit that demo?

00:48:26.160 --> 00:48:27.300
We've got 12 minutes
left on here.

00:48:27.300 --> 00:48:28.050
JOE CORKERY: Yeah.

00:48:28.050 --> 00:48:29.130
MICHAEL AMES: OK.

00:48:29.130 --> 00:48:31.815
So we just want to prove that
we're not making stuff up here.

00:48:31.815 --> 00:48:33.940
Here's our Twitter handle
if you want to follow us.

00:48:33.940 --> 00:48:35.340
We don't tweet a lot,
but there's stuff there.

00:48:35.340 --> 00:48:36.381
Maybe we'll make friends.

00:48:38.920 --> 00:48:41.880
Couple of screenshots
and then a running demo,

00:48:41.880 --> 00:48:45.060
if it works, of actual things
that we have actually built.

00:48:45.060 --> 00:48:49.560
Here is a dashboard in Tableau
that shows top 10 disease

00:48:49.560 --> 00:48:53.370
diagnoses for one of
our hospital systems,

00:48:53.370 --> 00:48:56.100
with lots of information about
the distribution across sex

00:48:56.100 --> 00:48:59.340
and race, co-morbidities,
distribution over patient age.

00:48:59.340 --> 00:49:03.060
And the fun thing is you
can click down on that list,

00:49:03.060 --> 00:49:06.570
and you can see this refresh,
and the numbers change.

00:49:06.570 --> 00:49:10.030
Scanning 133 million
diagnosis records,

00:49:10.030 --> 00:49:12.030
and it refreshes and does
all that recalculating

00:49:12.030 --> 00:49:14.789
in about 10 seconds.

00:49:14.789 --> 00:49:17.080
Here's a static copy of the
thing I'm going to show you

00:49:17.080 --> 00:49:21.400
that tells us a little bit
about how the flu is spreading

00:49:21.400 --> 00:49:24.790
in the Denver metro area, year
over year, and time over time.

00:49:24.790 --> 00:49:26.930
And I'm told that
for this to work,

00:49:26.930 --> 00:49:28.680
I have to come click on this.

00:49:31.830 --> 00:49:32.710
This works, right?

00:49:32.710 --> 00:49:33.370
That moves.

00:49:33.370 --> 00:49:33.911
Check it out.

00:49:33.911 --> 00:49:35.340
This is in Tableau.

00:49:35.340 --> 00:49:38.280
And you can see on
the left in that map,

00:49:38.280 --> 00:49:40.470
those dots are our
various clinics,

00:49:40.470 --> 00:49:43.020
and we're seeing the rate at
which the flu is spreading.

00:49:43.020 --> 00:49:45.220
And we're comparing
that year over year

00:49:45.220 --> 00:49:47.880
with each of those
columns on the right.

00:49:47.880 --> 00:49:50.970
Again, kudos to my
team and to our great

00:49:50.970 --> 00:49:53.040
BI developers and
analysts who figured out

00:49:53.040 --> 00:49:54.682
how to put this thing together.

00:49:54.682 --> 00:49:56.640
We're going to let that
flow for just a minute,

00:49:56.640 --> 00:49:58.681
because at the end something
really cool happens.

00:50:02.070 --> 00:50:04.110
One of the unexpected
side benefits

00:50:04.110 --> 00:50:06.240
of moving to Google
BigQuery is being

00:50:06.240 --> 00:50:08.190
able to take advantage
of Google's efforts

00:50:08.190 --> 00:50:12.107
to bring public data
sets into BigQuery.

00:50:12.107 --> 00:50:14.190
For those of you who haven't
worked with BigQuery,

00:50:14.190 --> 00:50:15.606
the interesting
thing is it's sort

00:50:15.606 --> 00:50:18.180
of like one great
big, shared DBMS.

00:50:18.180 --> 00:50:20.274
You have data, other
people have data,

00:50:20.274 --> 00:50:22.440
and assuming that the
permissions are set correctly,

00:50:22.440 --> 00:50:25.500
you can join across
to different data sets

00:50:25.500 --> 00:50:29.220
with literally one join
statement in your SQL command.

00:50:29.220 --> 00:50:32.880
So you'll see this thing
come to the end of its period

00:50:32.880 --> 00:50:34.590
here in just a minute.

00:50:34.590 --> 00:50:37.230
Look at that big spike in 2014.

00:50:37.230 --> 00:50:40.682
The scientists got the flu
vaccine wrong that year,

00:50:40.682 --> 00:50:42.390
and we're a little
bit nervous about what

00:50:42.390 --> 00:50:44.430
2016 is looking like.

00:50:44.430 --> 00:50:46.990
But now, just to see
what's possible here,

00:50:46.990 --> 00:50:51.630
the magical mouse is going
to move up, click a button.

00:50:51.630 --> 00:50:55.290
We're going to color code
those trends on the top

00:50:55.290 --> 00:50:57.570
according to weather.

00:50:57.570 --> 00:51:01.650
So that is blue when it's
cold, and red when it's warm.

00:51:01.650 --> 00:51:05.580
And that data was brought in
almost instantly with one join

00:51:05.580 --> 00:51:10.560
to a public data set from Google
that talks about daily weather

00:51:10.560 --> 00:51:11.910
station data.

00:51:11.910 --> 00:51:14.550
So we build real
stuff with this.

00:51:14.550 --> 00:51:15.840
And that's all I have.

00:51:15.840 --> 00:51:16.745
So thank you.

